// cffi U4 — VARARGS, arm 1: `snprintf(buf, 32, "%d-%d", 1, 2)` (plan §8 U4).
//
// The declaration's trailing `...` is what makes the extra arguments legal at
// all (R-11/R-12 count only the FIXED parameters, `buf`/`n`/`fmt`), and cffi
// U4 is what puts QBE's `...` marker in the emitted call:
//
//   %t1 =w call $snprintf(l %t0, l 32, l $d0, ..., l 1, l 2)
//                                             ^^^ HERE
//   %t2 =l extsw %t1
//
// WHY THE MARKER'S POSITION IS THE WHOLE UNIT: QBE derives the SysV
// vector-register count (`%al`) from the arguments AFTER the marker and emits
// `movl $<count>, %eax` before the call — MEASURED in this fixture's own
// assembly, `movl $0, %eax` / `callq snprintf`, and in a raw probe with a `d`
// among them, `movl $1, %eax` (plan §5).  A variadic callee's `va_start`
// prologue reads `%al` whatever the argument count, so the marker must be
// emitted even for a call that passes NO extra arguments at all.
//
// THE GOLDEN IS LOAD-BEARING ON THE ARGUMENTS, NOT ON THE MARKER, and that is
// stated rather than glossed: MEASURED by mutating the emitter to never emit
// the marker, `"1-2"` still prints (glibc's printf tolerates a stale `%al` when
// every variadic argument is word-class — which is exactly what v1 admits, so
// `%al` is ALWAYS 0 in the calls this compiler emits today).  The instrument
// that can FAIL is qbe-check.sh's run_midshape row, which pins the marker's
// position literally.  What the golden does prove is the other half: the
// declared boundary (`ptr`, `i64`, `ptr`, then `l` words) plus the `w` return
// and its extension produce the right string and the right length.
module Ffis
export Ffis.fmt : val -> val

extern snprintf(buf : ptr, n : i64, fmt : ptr, ...) -> i32;

defun Ffis.fmt(dummy : val) -> val {
  word buf = allocraw(32);
  word n = rawcall snprintf(buf, 32, rawstr("%d-%d"), 1, 2);
  return mkstr(buf, n);
}
